     1	# NPM shim integrity manifest
     2	
     3	> **Integrity contract for the `ceo-orchestration` npm shim.** The package is
     4	> built and versioned (`VERSION` / `package.json`, currently 1.0.1).
     5	> Publishing stays gated — `npm-publish.yml` holds all `v*-rc.*` tags and
     6	> requires manual approval on GA tags via `environment: production-npm`.
     7	> The integrity controls enforced **today** are: (1) an `install.sh`
     8	> self-SHA tamper trailer (`# CEO-INSTALL-SHA256:`) that re-hashes the
     9	> installer at install time and **fails closed** on mismatch; (2) build
    10	> provenance via `npm publish --provenance` (`publishConfig.provenance:
    11	> true`, a Sigstore / SLSA-Level-2 attestation); and (3) a per-tarball
    12	> SHA-256 manifest (`npm/SHA256SUMS.txt` plus a detached `<tarball>.sha256`)
    13	> recorded by `npm-publish.yml` at tag cut. The sections below detail each
    14	> control and the items that remain release-operator steps (GPG detached
    15	> signature, reproducible build) rather than automated gates.
    16	
    17	## Contract
    18	
    19	Every release tarball (`npm pack --dry-run` output) MUST satisfy:
    20	
    21	| Control | Value / mechanism | Where enforced |
    22	|---|---|---|
    23	| SHA-256 manifest per file | `sha256sum` over every file in `files:` array | `.github/workflows/validate.yml` (to-add) + manifest committed to `npm/SHA256SUMS.txt` during release prep |
    24	| GPG detached signature | RFC 4880 signature over tarball | Release operator signs locally with project key; signature attached to GitHub Release as `ceo-orchestration-<version>.tgz.asc` |
    25	| SLSA Level-2 provenance | `npm publish --provenance` (Sigstore-attested via OIDC) | `.github/workflows/npm-publish.yml` already passes `--provenance`; requires `id-token: write` permission (already set) |
    26	| Reproducible build | `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH` set to VERSION tag commit date | Release script (Sprint 17 scope) sets env var before `npm pack` |
    27	| Zero runtime dependencies | `Object.keys(dependencies).length === 0` | `.github/workflows/npm-publish.yml` step "Verify zero runtime dependencies" (existing) |
    28	| VERSION parity | `VERSION` file == `npm/package.json.version` == tag `v<version>` | `.github/workflows/npm-publish.yml` step "Verify VERSION matches tag" (existing) |
    29	
    30	## File-layer SHA-256 manifest (example)
    31	
    32	Generated during release prep, committed to `npm/SHA256SUMS.txt` adjacent to
    33	`package.json`:
    34	
    35	```
    36	<sha256>  bin/ceo-orch-init.js
    37	<sha256>  package.json
    38	<sha256>  scripts/install.sh
    39	<sha256>  templates/CLAUDE.md
    40	...
    41	```
    42	
    43	Consumers verify with:
    44	
    45	```bash
    46	cd $(npm root -g)/ceo-orchestration
    47	sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS.txt
    48	```
    49	
    50	## GPG key
    51	
    52	Project signing key fingerprint published in `docs/rotation-log.md` §NPM.
    53	Rotation: same 90-day maximum as other project credentials (ADR-040 §4).
    54	Public key distributed via GitHub Release notes + `.well-known/gpg.asc`.
    55	
    56	## SLSA Level-2 provenance
    57	
    58	`npm publish --provenance` attaches a Sigstore-signed attestation declaring:
    59	- Source repository (git URL)
    60	- Commit SHA
    61	- Workflow file that built the tarball
    62	- Builder (GitHub Actions runner identity)
    63	
    64	Consumers inspect via:
    65	
    66	```bash
    67	npm audit signatures ceo-orchestration
    68	```
    69	
    70	## Reproducible-build spec
    71	
    72	Inputs:
    73	- `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH = <VERSION tag creator-date, unix-epoch>`
    74	- Node 20.x (SHA-pinned in `npm-publish.yml`)
    75	- No `npm install` for the bundle itself (zero runtime deps)
    76	
    77	Expected output: byte-identical tarball across any ubuntu-latest GitHub
    78	Actions runner with the same inputs. Deviation = rebuild failure.
    79	
    80	## CI verification (npm pack --dry-run assertion)
    81	
    82	A new step in `validate.yml` asserts:
    83	
    84	```yaml
    85	- name: Assert npm pack produces expected bundle
    86	  run: |
    87	    cd npm
    88	    SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH=$(git log -1 --format=%ct) npm pack --dry-run > /tmp/pack-out.txt
    89	    grep -q "^filename:" /tmp/pack-out.txt || { echo "::error::npm pack did not report filename"; exit 1; }
    90	```
    91	
    92	The `--dry-run` variant does not publish; it only asserts the pack step would
    93	succeed. Full tarball signing + provenance is the release-operator workflow.
    94	
    95	## Not yet automated (release-operator or out of scope)
    96	
    97	- **GPG detached signature** is a manual release-operator step (sign the
    98	  tarball locally; attach the `.asc` to the GitHub Release), not a CI gate.
    99	- **Reproducible build** (`SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH`-pinned `npm pack`) is specified
   100	  above but not yet asserted byte-for-byte in CI.
   101	- **SLSA Level-3** (hermetic build + two-party review) is out of scope; the
   102	  shipped provenance is SLSA Level-2 (`--provenance`).
   103	
   104	## References
   105	
   106	- PLAN-013 Phase E.7 (this ADR source)
   107	- PLAN-013 Phase 0 item 0.2 — `npm-publish.yml` RC + manual-approval gates
   108	- ADR-040 §4 — credential lifecycle (90-day rotation applies to GPG key)
   109	- `.github/workflows/npm-publish.yml` — existing publish pipeline (gated)
   110	- `.github/workflows/validate.yml` — `npm pack --dry-run` assertion site
   111	- Sigstore + SLSA: <https://slsa.dev/spec/v1.0/levels>
   112	- npm provenance: <https://docs.npmjs.com/generating-provenance-statements>
     1	# ceo-orchestration
     2	
     3	<!-- last-reviewed: 2026-08-02 v1.2.0 -->
     4	
     5	> **Português:** [`README.pt-BR.md`](README.pt-BR.md)
     6	
     7	[![License: MIT](https://img.shields.io/badge/License-MIT-yellow.svg)](LICENSE)
     8	[![CI](https://github.com/Canhada-Labs/ceo-orchestration/actions/workflows/validate.yml/badge.svg)](https://github.com/Canhada-Labs/ceo-orchestration/actions/workflows/validate.yml)
     9	[![Release](https://img.shields.io/github/v/release/Canhada-Labs/ceo-orchestration)](https://github.com/Canhada-Labs/ceo-orchestration/releases)
    10	
    11	A governance and auditability layer for running [Claude Code](https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code) as a structured team of specialist agents — installed **into** an existing repository, not run as a service.
    12	
    13	> **Status:** maintained personal-use framework — bus-factor 1, no roadmap commitments; the public repo is a stable snapshot, not an actively-staffed product. There is no public community, contributor base, or external adopter to speak of — just the framework and its tests.
    14	
    15	The premise is narrow and deliberate: an LLM coding agent is most dangerous not when it writes bad code, but when it makes an **unreviewed, unrecorded, irreversible change** to a repository you care about. `ceo-orchestration` puts a gate and a ledger in front of that moment.
    16	
    17	**Who it's for:** a solo maintainer or small team running Claude Code on a repository they care about, who want governance and an audit trail over their agent's changes — not speed. If that isn't you, this will mostly feel like friction.
    18	
    19	---
    20	
    21	## No speed claim
    22	
    23	Six internal experiments found **no general speedup** from this orchestration over an optimized solo Claude Code session. The value here is **governance and auditability** — properties that are orthogonal to velocity. We say so at the top rather than dress it up. If you are shopping for "make my agent faster," this is the wrong tool.
    24	
    25	What it does give you:
    26	
    27	- A **Plan → Debate → Execute** gate for risky changes, so an agent cannot silently rewrite protected files.
    28	- A **tamper-evident audit log** (HMAC-chained) of every agent spawn (the CEO launching a subagent), edit, and ceremony — so you can *detect* after the fact whether the recorded history was altered.
    29	- A **cross-model review rail**: a second LLM (Codex) reviews Claude's edits to protected paths before they land.
    30	- A catalog of ready-made **skill checklists** an agent loads on demand instead of improvising.
    31	
    32	---
    33	
    34	## How it works
    35	
    36	When installed, the framework registers a set of [Claude Code hooks](https://docs.anthropic.com/en/docs/claude-code/hooks) that intercept tool calls (spawning agents, editing files, running shell commands) and apply policy *before* the action happens.
    37	
    38	**1. The CEO protocol.** Work is framed as a "CEO" delegating to specialist agents — that framing is where the name `ceo-orchestration` comes from. The session acts as a CEO that decides *what* needs doing and delegates each piece to a purpose-built agent (a security reviewer, an onboarding guide, a debate persona), rather than doing everything in one undifferentiated thread. Routine edits proceed directly. Cross-cutting or risky changes are gated: they require a recorded plan, and for the riskiest class, a structured multi-round debate among agent personas before execution. The protocol is a written contract (`PROTOCOL.md`), enforced mechanically by hooks rather than by convention.
    39	
    40	**2. Tamper-evident audit log.** Every governed event appends one JSONL line to a local audit log, each entry HMAC-chained to the previous one. A `verify_chain()` routine walks the log and reports the first break. This **detects tampering** — a removed or edited entry breaks the chain — but it does not *prove* the absence of tampering, and it is a local control, not a notarization service. Treat it as an integrity tripwire, not a court exhibit.
    41	
    42	**3. Cross-model pair-rail.** When an agent tries to edit a canonical (protected) path, a hook routes the proposed change to a second model for read-only review. If that reviewer returns anything write-shaped, the edit is blocked. The honest caveat: the default reviewer is another large language model, and same-class reviewers share blind spots (see *Risks*).
    43	
    44	**4. Skill checklists.** The framework ships **166 skill files** — reusable, domain-specific checklists (security review, audit fan-out, onboarding to an unfamiliar codebase, and so on) that an agent loads when relevant instead of reinventing the steps each time.
    45	
    46	---
    47	
    48	## What's in the box
    49	
    50	All counts below are verifiable from a clean checkout (see *Verifying the numbers*).
    51	
    52	| Component | Count | Notes |
    53	|---|---|---|
    54	| Skill checklists | **166** | 42 core + 8 frontend + 116 domain |
    55	| Hook scripts (on disk) | **57** | Python entrypoints under `.claude/hooks/` |
    56	| Hooks wired in `settings.json` | **46** | distinct scripts, 48 event registrations |
    57	| Shared library modules | **68** | stdlib-only, under `.claude/hooks/_lib/` (excluding the package `__init__.py`) |
    58	| Slash commands | **27** | under `.claude/commands/` |
    59	| Architecture decision records | **190** | under `.claude/adr/` |
    60	| Tests | **~14,000 cases** | reported by `pytest --collect-only` across the hook, script, and conformance suites |
    61	
    62	The gap between **57 on disk** and **46 wired** is benign: several non-event modules are activated through in-process dispatch (invoked by other hooks) rather than by a direct `settings.json` event registration.
    63	
    64	**Runtime dependencies: none.** Hooks and scripts are Python ≥ 3.9, **standard library only** — zero third-party runtime packages. See [`SBOM.md`](SBOM.md). (Development and CI use third-party test tooling such as pytest; the installed runtime does not.)
    65	
    66	There is also a **published compliance contract** under `SPEC/v1/` (32 files — 28 `*.schema.md` plus contract docs, version-pinned), a TLA+ specification of the core circuit-breaker state machine, a conformance harness, and a local read-only audit dashboard.
    67	
    68	---
    69	
    70	## Which skill should I use?
    71	
    72	166 skills is a discovery problem, not a feature. The shortlist below covers the everyday cases; everything else can wait until something breaks. Slash commands are typed in the Claude Code chat; bare names are skill checklists under `.claude/skills/core/` that you (or a spawned agent) load by name.
    73	
    74	| If you need… | Use | One-line why |
    75	|---|---|---|
    76	| A governed session start | `/ceo-boot` | Runs the session boot checks and prints a state digest before any work |
    77	| A single-glance picture of project state | `/status` | Plan, phase, vetoes, and recent audit activity in one screen |
    78	| To delegate work to a specialist agent | `/spawn "<Agent>" "<task>"` | Builds the persona + skill + file-assignment prompt the spawn hook enforces |
    79	| To gate a risky (L3+) change | `/debate start PLAN-NNN "<proposal>"` | Recorded multi-round debate among agent personas before execution |
    80	| A structured code review | `code-review-checklist` | The review checklist a spawned reviewer loads instead of improvising |
    24	      serial quebra.
    25	- [ ] `bash .claude/scripts/validate-governance.sh` exit 0
    26	- [ ] `bash .claude/scripts/check-contamination.sh` exit 0
    27	
    28	### Documentação
    29	
    30	- [ ] `CLAUDE.md` §Current Work reflete o estado atual
    31	- [ ] `CHANGELOG.md` tem entry pro release mais recente
    32	- [ ] Coverage gate ENFORCING verde (`.github/workflows/coverage.yml`)
    33	- [ ] `docs/actions-versions.md` tem SHAs atualizados
    34	- [ ] `VERSION` file coerente com a tag: em tag **`-rc.N` o VERSION fica
    35	      PURO** (`X.Y.Z`, sem sufixo — o `release.yml` strippa `-rc.N` na
    36	      validação); em tag stable, idêntico. E o bump NÃO é 1 site. Os
    37	      sites que `verify-counts.sh` de fato checa (§VERSION_SITES + os
    38	      lidos à parte), mais o `VERSION` que é a fonte da verdade:
    39	      `VERSION` (fonte) ·
    40	      `INSTALL.md` (`--pin vX.Y.Z`) ·
    41	      `docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` (`currently vX.Y.Z, aligned with the repo`) ·
