     1	---
     2	description: Owner-invoked autonomy posture toggle — hands you the exact command to arm acceptEdits for the NEXT session, reversibly and audited — /night-mode on|off|status
     3	argument-hint: "on|off|status"
     4	allowed-tools: Read
     5	---
     6	
     7	# /night-mode — Owner autonomy posture toggle (PLAN-165)
     8	
     9	Usage: `/night-mode on`, `/night-mode off`, or `/night-mode status`.
    10	
    11	Arms or disarms a per-machine autonomy posture: `on` sets
    12	`permissions.defaultMode: "acceptEdits"` in the gitignored
    13	`.claude/settings.local.json` overlay; `off` restores the snapshotted
    14	prior value. The tracked `.claude/settings.json` (the published
    15	fail-closed default, `defaultMode: "manual"`) is **never touched**. All
    16	logic lives in `.claude/scripts/night-mode.py`.
    17	
    18	## ⛔ This command does NOT run the toggle — you do
    19	
    20	**Owner-ratified OQ1-redo (2026-08-03): arming or disarming the autonomy
    21	posture is a HUMAN action.** The model rail does not flip the posture of
    22	your next session; a human at the keyboard does.
    23	
    24	**What actually enforces this, without overclaiming** (codex S292 r7 P1 —
    25	the earlier wording said "denies every model-rail invocation", which is
    26	false and measurably so):
    27	
    28	- **Closed, both rails:** Edit/Write of the writer, the overlay and the
    29	  marker (`.claude/settings.json` deny + `_CANONICAL_GUARDS`, which the
    30	  Bash rail keys off). A tool write or a shell redirect cannot touch them.
    31	- **Best-effort, not a boundary:** `check_bash_safety`
    32	  (`_e4_check_posture_toggle_invocation`, PLAN-165 NF-08) blocks the
    33	  invocation shapes a STATIC shell parse can recognise — and static
    34	  parsing of bash is not complete. A RENAMED INTERPRETER
    35	  (`./zz-interp .claude/scripts/night-mode.py on`) is measured to pass
    36	  both the matcher and the script's self-path guard, because the script
    37	  that ends up running IS the canonical one
    38	  (`nf08-invocation-guard-NOTES.md` §7 residual 5).
    39	- **Therefore:** a session with broad Bash permission can still arm the
    40	  next session's posture. These controls raise the cost and make the
    41	  honest path obvious; they do not make it impossible. The real perimeter
    42	  is the CURRENT session's `defaultMode` + Bash allowlist.
    43	
    44	Reading the script and its state (cat/grep/git) stays open.
    45	
    46	So this command is a **teleprompter, not a driver**. Claude's whole job
    47	here is to hand you one line to run yourself.
    48	
    49	### How Claude must respond
    50	
    51	1. Parse the first token of `$ARGUMENTS` as the subcommand. Accept
    52	   case-insensitive `on` / `off` / `status`. Default (no argument) =
    53	   `status`. Reject any other token with a short usage line — do not
    54	   guess.
    55	2. Print the matching command from the sections below, prefixed with
    56	   `!` so the operator can run it on the human rail of this session
    57	   (the `!` prefix executes in the terminal, not through the model's
    58	   Bash tool). A terminal outside Claude Code works identically.
    59	3. Print the one-line reminder that goes with that subcommand.
    60	4. **Stop.** Do not call Bash. Do not "helpfully" verify by running the
    61	   script. If the operator pastes the output back, read it and explain
    62	   it — that is the whole loop.
    63	
    64	## Next-session semantics (the whole point)
    65	
    66	Settings are read at **session start**. The toggle therefore takes
    67	effect on the **next** session, never the current one:
    68	
    69	> Turn it on before you go to sleep → the overnight session starts in
    70	> `acceptEdits` → turn it off in the morning.
    71	
    72	The currently running governed session never changes posture under the
    73	operator. If you need autonomy *now*, start a new session after `on`.
    74	
    75	## `on` — arm night mode
    76	
    77	Command for the operator to run:
    78	
    79	```
    80	! python3 .claude/scripts/night-mode.py on
    81	```
    82	
    83	Reminder to print with it:
    84	`night-mode: takes effect on the NEXT session; run /night-mode off in the morning.`
    85	
    86	The script (single writer — direct Edit/Write of the overlay is denied
    87	by policy):
    88	
    89	1. Merge-writes `permissions.defaultMode: "acceptEdits"` into
    90	   `.claude/settings.local.json` (lock → atomic temp+`os.replace` →
    91	   read-back; malformed existing JSON is fail-CLOSED: non-zero exit,
    92	   file untouched).
    93	2. Snapshots the prior value **create-only** into the marker so a
    94	   double `on` can never make the weak posture the thing `off`
    95	   "restores".
    96	3. Writes the marker+snapshot file `.claude/state/night-mode.json`
    97	   (gitignored via `.claude/state/`) with timestamp, hostname, and the
    98	   mode it wrote.
    99	4. Emits the `night_mode_toggled` audit action (HMAC-chained).
   100	
   101	`on` twice is an idempotent no-op (exit 0). The script **refuses to run
   102	when `CI` is set** — this is an operator/local-machine control only.
   103	
   104	## `off` — disarm night mode
   105	
   106	```
   107	! python3 .claude/scripts/night-mode.py off
   108	```
   109	
   110	Restores the snapshotted `defaultMode` (or removes the key if none
   111	existed), then removes the marker — reverse order of `on`, so a crash
   112	in between is detectable, never silent. `off` twice is an idempotent
   113	no-op (exit 0). Takes effect on the next session.
   114	
   115	The marker is validated as a **whole document** before any of its fields
   116	is acted on (version, `mode_written`, both booleans, `prev_value` against
   117	a closed set derived from the harness's real `--permission-mode` enum,
   118	and the `created_file`/`prev_present` consistency rule). Anything that a
   119	healthy `on` could not have written is fail-CLOSED: exit 2, overlay
   120	untouched, marker left in place as evidence. `bypassPermissions` and
   121	`acceptEdits` are **never** restorable values.
   122	
   123	### `off --discard-snapshot` — recovery from a refusal
   124	
   125	```
   126	! python3 .claude/scripts/night-mode.py off --discard-snapshot
   127	```
   128	
   129	The sanctioned exit from **every** fail-closed refusal above: it removes
   130	the local `defaultMode` override **and** the marker *without* honoring
   131	`prev_value`, printing exactly what it discarded. A refusal must never
   132	leave the posture armed with no way to disarm it.
   133	
   134	- It also disarms the **armed-without-marker** state, where plain `off`
   135	  is a no-op (crash between the two writes, or a hand-deleted marker).
   136	- It removes **one key, never a file** — an overlay carrying your other
   137	  settings survives with `defaultMode` stripped.
   138	- It is still fail-CLOSED on a malformed overlay (it must rewrite that
   139	  file; repair the JSON by hand first).
   140	- The next session resolves the project layer's ratified posture. If you
   141	  wanted a specific prior value back, set it yourself after recovering —
   142	  discard deliberately does not trust the snapshot it just threw away.
   143	
   144	## `status` — report resolved posture
   145	
   146	```
   147	! python3 .claude/scripts/night-mode.py status
   148	```
   149	
   150	Prints:
   151	
   152	- the **resolved** `permissions.defaultMode` per layer, via
   153	  `_lib/effective_config.resolve_settings()` — the same resolver the
   154	  tamper tripwires use, with layer provenance (which layer won:
   155	  project vs local);
   156	- the marker's age and contents (when armed, by whom/where);
   157	- whether the marker and the resolved configuration **agree**.
   158	
   159	Truth comes from the resolver, not the marker. The marker is
   160	decoration.
   161	
   162	`status` is read-only, and it is denied to the model rail anyway: the
   163	invocation guard keys off the *script*, not the subcommand, because a
   164	permissive carve-out driven by argv parsing is exactly the shape the
   165	fail-closed doctrine rejects. Claude does have a model-rail read of the
   166	same fact — **`/ceo-boot`'s advisory night-mode banner is
   167	resolver-derived** and needs no subprocess. Use that when Claude needs
   168	to *know* the posture; use `! … status` when you want the full report.
   169	
   170	## Escape valve — real bypass is NOT this command
   171	
   172	Night-mode tops out at `acceptEdits`. It never writes
   173	`bypassPermissions` — that value trips the `settings_tamper_tripwires`
   174	red in `/ceo-boot` by design, in any layer (PLAN-165 D1). If you truly
   175	need a full-bypass session, the honest path is one-shot and explicit:
   176	
   177	```
   178	claude --permission-mode bypassPermissions   # one session, explicit, ephemeral
   179	```
   180	
   181	No persistent state, no tripwire collision, nothing for morning-you to
   182	forget. Never route bypass through night-mode.
   183	
   184	## Acceptance invariant — tracked tree stays clean
   185	
   186	After any subcommand, `git status --porcelain` must be empty (AC-1).
   187	Both files the toggle touches are gitignored:
   188	`.claude/settings.local.json` and `.claude/state/night-mode.json`. If
   189	the tree is dirty after a toggle, something wrote outside the contract
   190	— stop and investigate before committing anything.
   191	
   192	## Troubleshooting
   193	
   194	- **"Claude refused to run it for me"** — by design (OQ1-redo). Run the
   195	  `!` line yourself; paste the output back if you want it read.
   196	- **"I ran `on` but the session still prompts"** — the toggle is
   197	  next-session; the running session keeps its boot posture. Start a
   198	  new session.
   199	- **Marker and settings disagree** (e.g. crash between the two writes,
   200	  or the overlay was hand-edited): `status` reports the disagreement
   201	  instead of picking a side. If the marker is **gone** but the overlay is
   202	  still armed, plain `off` is a no-op — run
   203	  `off --discard-snapshot` to strip the override, then `on` again if you
   204	  still want night mode. Never "fix" it by running `off` then `on` in
   205	  that state: `on` would snapshot the armed value.
   206	- **`off` refuses with "not a healthy night-mode marker"** — the marker
   207	  is gitignored, unguarded state and something outside night-mode wrote
   208	  it (a tampered `prev_value` such as `bypassPermissions` or
   209	  `acceptEdits`, a non-boolean field, a wrong `version`, or an
   210	  inconsistent `created_file`/`prev_present` pair). Validation is
   211	  fail-CLOSED: the overlay is untouched and the marker is kept as
   212	  evidence. Recover with:
   213	
   214	  ```
   215	  ! python3 .claude/scripts/night-mode.py off --discard-snapshot
   216	  ```
   217	
   218	  This removes the override and the marker without honoring the
   219	  snapshot, and prints what it discarded. Keep a copy of the marker
   220	  first if you want to investigate who wrote it — the PostToolUse audit
   221	  log records tool writes to it.
   222	- **Non-zero exit with a parse diagnostic** — your existing
   223	  `settings.local.json` is malformed JSON. The script will not rewrite
   224	  or "repair" it (fail-closed on input). Fix the JSON by hand, re-run.
   225	- **Refused under CI** — expected: night-mode is operator/local only.
   226	- **What actually applies?** — trust `status`'s layer provenance line;
   227	  it uses the official resolver, not a re-implementation.
   228	
   229	## Exit codes
   230	
   231	- 0 — success (including idempotent no-ops)
   232	- non-zero — refused (CI set), malformed input, tampered/invalid marker,
   233	  or read-back mismatch; the settings file and marker are left in a
   234	  diagnosable state. Any refusal is recoverable with
   235	  `off --discard-snapshot` — no refusal can strand the posture armed.
     1	#!/usr/bin/env python3
     2	"""night-mode — Owner-invoked autonomy posture toggle (PLAN-165 W1 T1.1).
     3	
     4	`/night-mode on|off|status` flips the per-machine autonomy posture by
     5	merge-writing `permissions.defaultMode: "acceptEdits"` into the gitignored
     6	`<project>/.claude/settings.local.json` overlay. The tracked
     7	`.claude/settings.json` (fail-closed default: `defaultMode: "manual"` +
     8	`disableAutoMode: "disable"`) is NEVER touched — settings are read at
     9	session start, so the toggle takes effect on the NEXT session
    10	("arm before sleep, disarm in the morning" semantics, PLAN-165 fact #5).
    11	
    12	## Design decisions carried in from the PLAN-165 v2 review
    13	
    14	- **D1 — no `bypassPermissions`, anywhere.** The only mode `on` ever
    15	  writes is `acceptEdits`, and `off` restores only values from the
    16	  closed set `_RESTORABLE_MODES` — which NEVER includes
    17	  `bypassPermissions` (NM-01). `bypassPermissions` in ANY settings layer
    18	  trips `TAMPER_PERMISSION_BYPASS` (`_lib/effective_config.py`) and turns
    19	  `/ceo-boot` red by design; the honest escape valve is an explicit,
    20	  ephemeral `claude --permission-mode bypassPermissions` session.
    21	- **D2 — single-writer via ceremony prerequisite P1.** A deny rule for
    22	  Edit/Write on `.claude/settings.local.json` (sentinel ceremony, blocks
    23	  W1 landing) makes this script — a process write, not a tool write —
    24	  the only writer of the overlay. This script does not and cannot
    25	  enforce P1 itself; it relies on it for the escalation-ladder closure.
    26	- **D3 — the boot banner derives from the resolver, not the marker.**
    27	  The marker/snapshot file is decoration + restore-state only. `status`
    28	  reconciles marker vs `_lib.effective_config.resolve_settings()` and
    29	  REPORTS disagreement instead of picking a side.
    30	
    31	## State model (W0 decisions — fixed, do not revisit)
    32	
    33	Marker AND snapshot live in ONE file, `<project>/.claude/state/night-mode.json`
    34	(gitignored via the `.claude/state/` entry), same filesystem as the
    35	settings overlay (atomicity; kills the slug-convention ambiguity, W0 T0.7):
    36	
    37	    {"version": 1, "mode_written": "acceptEdits", "prev_present": bool,
    38	     "prev_value": null | <mode in _RESTORABLE_MODES>,
    39	     "created_file": bool, "ts": "...Z", "hostname": "..."}
    40	
    41	The file is gitignored, unguarded and agent-writable, so on the way IN
    42	(`off`) it is UNTRUSTED input: `_validate_marker` checks the whole
    43	document against that schema — including the consistency rule
    44	`created_file=True implies prev_present=False` — before any field is
    45	acted on (NF-03).
    46	
    47	The snapshot is CREATE-ONLY (contract item 4): if the marker exists, `on`
    48	is a no-op (exit 0). Without this, a second `on` would snapshot the value
    49	night-mode itself wrote and `off` would "restore" `acceptEdits` — a
    50	permanently weakened posture.
    51	
    52	## Write contract (PLAN-165 §Contrato de escrita — all items mandatory)
    53	
    54	1. `_lib.filelock.FileLock` around every whole mutation sequence.
    55	2. Atomic writes only: tempfile in the SAME directory, flush + fsync,
    56	   `os.replace`. Never a truncating in-place write — a torn/truncated
    57	   settings file makes the harness skip the WHOLE file (S286 class).
    58	3. Read-back after replace: reopen + re-parse; mismatch exits non-zero
    59	   WITHOUT touching the marker.
    60	4. Snapshot is create-only (above).
    61	5. Defined ordering: `on` writes settings THEN marker; `off` restores
    62	   settings THEN removes the marker. `status` reconciles and reports.
    63	6. Malformed existing input is fail-CLOSED (exit 2, no write, clear
    64	   diagnostic) — repo doctrine: fail-open on infra, fail-closed on input.
    65	7. Double-`on` / double-`off` are no-ops that exit 0.
    66	
    67	## Non-goals with enforcement
    68	
    69	No autonomy in CI: `on`/`off` REFUSE (exit 2) whenever the `CI` env var
    70	is present (even empty — fail-closed on presence). `status` stays
    71	available read-only. No tty gate (W0 T0.6: the Bash rail has no tty;
    72	Owner presence is guaranteed by P1 + the CI refusal).
    73	
    74	## Audit (NM-05 / NF-07 — two records, one per terminating path)
    75	
    76	Every `on`/`off` invocation leaves TWO records on EVERY terminating
    77	path, written as a pair, never one without the other:
    78	
    79	1. ONE machine-readable summary line on stdout (stable key=value shape) —
    80	   the operator-facing record, unsigned and terminal-scoped:
    81	
    82	       night-mode-event mode=... previous_mode=... result=applied|noop|refused|failed
    83	
    84	2. ONE `night_mode_toggled` row on the HMAC-chained audit log, via
    85	   `_emit_audit` -> `_lib.audit_emit.emit_night_mode_toggled` — the
    86	   FORENSIC record PLAN-165 AC-7 requires. The overlay is written by a
    87	   PROCESS, not by a tool, so no PostToolUse observer covers it: without
    88	   this row a posture flip between sessions leaves no signed trace.
    89	
    90	The action is REGISTERED (`_KNOWN_ACTIONS`, typed wrapper, deny-by-default
    91	scrub branch, SPEC row — sentinel ceremony `610d9ec`), so the emit is
    92	live. Only the four allowlisted fields travel (`mode`, `previous_mode`,
    93	`result`, `hostname_hash`); never a path, never file content, never the
    94	raw hostname. The emit is best-effort and NEVER raises: observability
    95	must not be able to block or fail the toggle.
    96	
    97	`status` emits NEITHER record — it is read-only, not a toggle.
    98	
    99	## Round-2 security hardening (see PLAN-165/architect/round-2/)
   100	
   101	- NM-01/NM-10 — `off` restores `prev_value` only if it is a string in
   102	  the closed set `_RESTORABLE_MODES`; anything else (wrong type, unknown
   103	  string, `bypassPermissions`) is fail-CLOSED exit 2 with the marker
   104	  left in place. A tampered marker can no longer launder a value into
   105	  the overlay through the Owner's own `off`.
   106	- NM-02 — `on` never snapshots night-mode's own value: if the overlay
   107	  already carries `acceptEdits` with no marker (crash/desync end-state),
   108	  the snapshot is normalized to "no prior override" so `off` restores
   109	  the project posture instead of freezing the weak one.
   110	- NM-04 — `--project-root` is confined to this repository unless the
   111	  test-only `CEO_NIGHT_MODE_TEST_SEAM` env var is set; the target must
   112	  already contain `.claude/settings.json`.
   113	- NM-09 — best-effort parent-directory fsync after `os.replace` and
   114	  after overlay/marker unlink.
   115	
   116	## Round-3 security hardening (findings NF-01..NF-04)
   117	
   118	- NF-01 — `_RESTORABLE_MODES` excludes `NIGHT_MODE` itself. `cmd_on`'s
   119	  desync normalization guarantees a healthy marker never carries
   120	  night-mode's own value, so admitting it could only ever admit a
   121	  TAMPERED value — and honoring it re-armed `acceptEdits` while REMOVING
   122	  the marker, leaving the posture armed with every later `off` a forever
   123	  no-op. `cmd_off` applies the same normalization symmetrically as a
   124	  second layer.
   125	- NF-02 — the restorable set is DERIVED from
   126	  `_HARNESS_PERMISSION_MODES`, the CLI's real `--permission-mode` enum,
   127	  instead of hand-listed: the hand-listed set carried a mode the harness
   128	  does not have (`default`) and omitted one it does (`dontAsk`), so a
   129	  legitimate `dontAsk` overlay was snapshotted by `on` and then refused
   130	  by `off` FOREVER with `acceptEdits` left armed — fail-closed landing on
   131	  the permissive side. `off --discard-snapshot` is the documented,
   132	  non-restoring recovery from every fail-closed refusal, referenced in
   133	  every refusal diagnostic.
   134	- NF-03 — `_validate_marker` validates the marker as a WHOLE DOCUMENT
   135	  before ANY field is acted on (version, mode_written, both booleans,
   136	  prev_value, and the `created_file=True implies prev_present=False`
   137	  consistency rule). Validating `prev_value` alone left the overlay
   138	  `os.unlink` branch reachable from a planted `created_file` — silent
   139	  data loss on an exit-0 `result=applied` path.
   140	- NF-04 — the one-summary-per-terminating-path requirement (and the P2
   141	  audit emit that lands beside it) is asserted STRUCTURALLY by an `ast`
   142	  oracle in `tests/test_night_mode.py`, not just described in prose.
   143	
   144	## Round-4 security hardening (findings NF-05..NF-06)
   145	
   146	One class, two surfaces: values from the two gitignored documents
   147	(`settings.local.json`, `.claude/state/night-mode.json`) were echoed into
   148	LINE-ORIENTED records without collapsing line breaks, so whoever can write
   149	either file forges whole extra lines in records the Owner and parsers
   150	trust.
   151	
   152	- NF-05 — `cmd_on`'s success line interpolated `previous` (the overlay's
   153	  RAW `permissions.defaultMode`) with neither `_bounded_repr` nor
   154	  `_summary_token`, the only untrusted echo in the file that did. A `\n`
   155	  in that value made ONE `on` emit TWO `night-mode-event ` rows, the
   156	  forged one claiming `result=applied`; a 200k-char value emitted 200k
   157	  bytes. It now goes through `_bounded_repr` like every sibling.
   158	- NF-06 — `cmd_status` never ran `_validate_marker` and printed
   159	  `host=` / `ts=` raw, and `_validate_marker` never checked those two
   160	  fields at all. So a planted `hostname` injected arbitrary status lines
   161	  (a forged `reconciliation:` verdict landing BEFORE the true one), and
   162	  `status` reported "AGREE — night-mode ON" for the very marker `off`
   163	  refuses with exit 2 — the reporter blessing what the writer rejects.
   164	  Now: `ts`/`hostname` are type-, length- and control-char-checked as part
   165	  of the whole document (`_invalid_bounded_string`), `status` RUNS the
   166	  validator and renders "PRESENT but INVALID (<field>)" with a DISAGREE
   167	  verdict for a rejected marker, every untrusted echo is bounded, and the
   168	  report is emitted through `_one_line` so no future field can forge a
   169	  record.
   170	
   171	## Round-3 RE-REVIEW hardening (findings NF-07, NF-09)
   172	
   173	- NF-07 — the `night_mode_toggled` action was registered, SPEC'd and
   174	  documented in signed surfaces, with ZERO production callers: a
   175	  registered-and-empty action, and three published claims that were
   176	  false. `_emit_audit` is now the sibling of every `_summary` call (see
   177	  `## Audit` above), so the signed claim "emitted on EVERY terminating
   178	  path of on/off" is true of this build. The `ast` oracle pins the PAIR,
   179	  not just the summary — a later refactor that drops one of the two is a
   180	  test failure, not a silent regression.
   181	- NF-09 — `off` on the route where the overlay had ALREADY been removed
   182	  by hand wrote nothing, then reported `restored to '<snapshot>'` with
   183	  `result=applied` on both records. It now reports `absent` / `noop` and
   184	  says plainly that nothing was restored. The bug was harmless while the
   185	  record was only a stdout line; with NF-07 wired it would have put a
   186	  false restore into the HMAC chain, which is why the two fixes belong in
   187	  one commit.
   188	
   189	- NF-08b — the script refuses to run unless the file it was invoked as IS
   190	  `<repo>/.claude/scripts/night-mode.py`. Round 2 of the cross-vendor
   191	  review found that the Bash matcher guarding this toggle recognises
   192	  SPELLINGS, and an alias exists to have a different one: `ln -s
   193	  <repo>/.claude/scripts/night-mode.py /tmp/nm && python3 /tmp/nm on`
   194	  armed the posture (measured, `result=applied`), because
   195	  `Path(__file__).resolve()` follows the link back into the repository.
   196	  Re-probing widened the class — a renamed copy inside the repo
   197	  (`.claude/scripts/nm2.py`) armed it too, and no rule about `ln` would
   198	  have caught that. The guard is here rather than in the hook because it
   199	  holds however the alias was created and even if the hook never runs;
   200	  the hook keeps the cheap half (denying `ln` on the toggle) as the
   201	  earlier signal. An ancestor directory being a symlink is NOT an alias
   202	  (`/tmp` -> `/private/tmp`), so ancestors are resolved separately.
   203	
   204	Exit codes:
   205	    0 — success or idempotent no-op
   206	    1 — read-back mismatch / post-write verification failure
   207	    2 — usage error, CI refusal, alias/relocated invocation (NF-08b),
   208	        root confinement (NM-04), tampered or malformed marker (NM-01 /
   209	        NF-01..NF-03 / NF-06), or malformed input (all fail-closed).
   210	        Recover an armed posture from any of these with
   211	        `off --discard-snapshot`.
   212	
   213	Stdlib-only; Python >= 3.9.
   214	"""
   215	
   216	from __future__ import annotations
   217	
   218	import argparse
   219	import copy
   220	import hashlib
   221	import json
   222	import os
   223	import socket
   224	import stat
   225	import sys
   226	import tempfile
